<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hemu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1501">1501</a>–<a href="/years/1556">1556</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Hem Chandra Vikramaditya, Samrat Hem Chandra Vikramaditya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hemu was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> general and minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sur-empire">Sur Empire</a> who rose from a merchant background to command the armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shah">Adil Shah</a> Suri. After winning many battles against Afghan rivals and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>, he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> in 1556 and proclaimed himself king as Vikramaditya. Weeks later he was wounded by an arrow at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-panipat">Second Battle of Panipat</a>, captured and executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hemu.jpg" alt="Hemu" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1602-03 artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hemu_captured_by_Akbar%27s_forces_in_1556.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Contemporary accounts of Hemu's early life are incomplete because of his humble background, and the Mughal historians Bada'uni and Abu'l-Fazl, who served at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s court, were often biased. It is generally accepted that he was born into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> family of limited means and spent his childhood in Rewari, south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, and he worked young as a trader in saltpetre.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sher-shah-suri">Sher Shah Suri</a> died in 1545, his son Islam Shah ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sur-empire">Sur Empire</a>, and Hemu became superintendent of the market in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. Islam Shah liked to place <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> in command alongside Afghan officers so that they could spy on each other, and he gave Hemu the responsibilities of a high-ranking officer. Islam Shah died on 30 October 1553, and his 12-year-old son Firoz Shah was killed within three days by his uncle Adil Shah Suri. Hemu backed Adil Shah and rose to chief minister, and Abu'l-Fazl wrote that he undertook all appointments and dismissals and the distribution of justice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Hemu is reputed to have won 22 battles for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adil-shah">Adil Shah</a>, many of them against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> who had rebelled. He defeated Taj Khan Karrani at Chibramau and again at Chunar, though Karrani escaped each time. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humayun">Humayun</a> died on 26 January 1556, Hemu marched rapidly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayana">Bayana</a>, Etawah, Sambhal, Kalpi and Narnaul, and the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a> fled without a fight.</p><p>Tardi Beg Khan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a>'s governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, met him at Tughlaqabad. According to Bada'uni, Hemu's army included 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a> and 50,000 cavalry. The Mughal van and left wing drove back the enemy, but Hemu charged with a reserve of 300 elephants and picked horsemen, and the Mughal officers fled. He took Delhi on 7 October 1556 and assumed the title of Vikramaditya. Historians disagree about what this meant: Satish Chandra doubts that Hemu declared himself independent, while others, citing Ahmad Yadgar, say that he raised the royal canopy and had coin struck in his name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 5 November 1556 the Mughal army met Hemu at Panipat. He led his army on an elephant, and the battle was turning in his favour when a Mughal arrow wounded him in the eye and he collapsed unconscious. His army panicked and broke, and about 5,000 dead lay on the field. Bairam Khan asked the 13-year-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> to behead the captive Hemu, and according to the contemporary Muhammad Arif Qandhari he did so and took the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazi">Ghazi</a>. Hemu's head was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> and his body was gibbeted on a gate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. Abu'l-Fazl praised his spirit, courage and enterprise. Supporters erected a memorial to him at Panipat, known as Hemu's Samadhi Sthal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hemu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>North of Delhi, the army of the young Mughal emperor Akbar, under his regent Bairam Khan, faced <b>Hemu</b>, who had taken Delhi weeks earlier.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-5">5 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Islam Shah continued centralised rule, while <b>Hemu</b>, a Hindu general, served his successors.</blockquote><a href="/state/sur-empire">Sur Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Alwar, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Panipat, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=27.57,76.62&amp;year=1536" aria-label="Alwar on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6562 1153 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hemu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7012 1423h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7012 1423h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=27.57,76.62&amp;year=1536">What was here in 1536? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
